{
  "dom_id": "step-q175383",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $a+b$ is an odd number,\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\{(a,b)\\,|\\,a=2,3,\\,b=5,8\\}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\{2,3\\}\\times \\{5,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{(2,5),(2,8),(3,5),(3,8)\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $(a,b)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $a+b$ is an odd number\n\nThe following pairs give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $(2,5)$ since $2+5=7$ and $7$ is an odd number,\n\n- $(3,8)$ since $3+8=11$ and $11$ is an odd number.\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=\\{(2,5),(3,8)\\}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
